INT. COUNTY ARCHIVE - READING ROOM - RAINY AFTERNOON

High windows, streaked with rain. Oak tables. Green-shaded lamps. The hush of paper.

THEODORA "TEDDY" BRANDT (40s, cardigan, a pencil behind her ear) turns the pages of a bound ledger with white cotton gloves. At the next table, EZEKIEL FAIRBANKS (60s, tweed, a magnifying glass on a cord) reads a yellowed newspaper. And behind the desk, the archivist MIRIAM ACHTERBERG (50s, bun, reading glasses on her forehead) sorts index cards.

Teddy stops on a page. Lifts a corner with her pencil. Looks again.

She glances at Ezekiel. He is watching her over the top of his newspaper. They both look away.

MIRIAM
(to the room)
Reading room closes at five, everyone.

Neither responds.

Teddy photographs the page with her phone, the shutter click loud in the silence. Miriam's eyes flick up, then down. She files a card.

Ezekiel rises, stretches, and strolls to Teddy's table. He glances at the ledger. His face stays perfectly polite.

EZEKIEL
Interesting hand. Eighteen-nineties?

TEDDY
Eighteen-ninety-four.

EZEKIEL
Mm. I hear the county clerk was a drinker.

TEDDY
I hear a lot of things.

He nods. Returns to his table. Sits. Turns a page of his newspaper without reading it.

Teddy takes a slow breath and closes the ledger. She slips off one glove, then the other, and folds them over the cover.

MIRIAM
(to herself)
Someone always takes that one.

Teddy looks up. Miriam keeps sorting.

TEDDY
Which one?

MIRIAM
The hand.

TEDDY
You've seen it?

MIRIAM
It's gone out four times this year.

Ezekiel's newspaper lowers a fraction. Rain presses at the glass.

TEDDY
Who else?

MIRIAM
I can't say. Patron privacy.

TEDDY
Of course.

Teddy gathers her things. Stands. Walks to the desk and slides the ledger across.

TEDDY (CONT'D)
Thank you.

MIRIAM
Come again.

TEDDY
I'll try.

She leaves. The door sighs shut behind her.

Ezekiel folds his newspaper, tucks it under his arm, and approaches the desk.

EZEKIEL
The same one, please.

Miriam lifts the ledger. Holds it for a moment.

MIRIAM
It's very popular today.

She passes it over. He opens it to the marked page. Smiles very slightly. Takes out his own phone.
